#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1020.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088811} :activity/id #uuid "f8700d0a-d600-5dce-b743-5464f739c710" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048130 :topic/id #uuid "81ceba80-b7bc-5614-b160-458b9437b8ca" :topic/math-academy-id 881} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088811 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088946 :tutorial/content "Recall that a second-order homogeneous differential equation takes the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $a(x),$ $b(x),$ and $c(x)$ are functions of $x$ only.\n\nA **second-order inhomogeneous** differential equation is similar, except the right-hand side does *not* equal zero. So, a second-order inhomogeneous differential equation can be written as\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nwhere $a(x),$ $b(x),$ and $c(x)$ are functions of $x$ only and $f(x)$ is a nonzero function.\n\nFor example, the following equation is a second-order inhomogeneous ODE because the right-hand side is $3$ instead of $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6y =3\n$$\n\nIn general, we say that a second-order ODE has **polynomial forcing** if $f(x)$ is a polynomial." :tutorial/id #uuid "6e9adc7d-336d-5b0f-96ca-a19f64b5714f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4061 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4969b0a4-b67d-5988-bc46-595ff3f801e8" :step/math-academy-id 594357 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088812}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088953 :tutorial/content "To find the general solution to an inhomogeneous ODE, we perform the following steps:\n\n- **Step 1:** Find the solution $y_c$ of the associated homogeneous ODE. This solution is called the **complementary solution** (or the **homogeneous solution**).\n\n- **Step 2:** Find a **particular solution** $y_p$ (also known as the **particular integral**) of the inhomogeneous ODE. If $f(x)$ is an $n$th degree polynomial, then so is a particular solution.\n\n- **Step 3:** Write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution: $y = y_c + y_p.$\n\nTo illustrate, let's find the general solution to the following inhomogeneous ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6y =3\n$$\n\n- **Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} x^2} + 5\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 6y = 0\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation (or auxiliary equation) is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)(\\lambda +2) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso $\\lambda=-3$ or $\\lambda=-2.$ Consequently, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{-3x} + Be^{-2x}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right-hand side is a constant, it is a polynomial of degree $0,$ which means a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $0,$ or a constant. So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be found. Calculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 0,\\quad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+5(0)+6\\alpha =3\\,⇒\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3:** Finally, we write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{-2x}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "632695f2-f752-5080-b0e9-8a624d205221" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4136 :tutorial/title "Solving Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODEs"} :step/id #uuid "002aa299-2816-508f-a9df-3570e71ccec6" :step/math-academy-id 594358 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088813}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087873 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087609 :question/id #uuid "83788240-c717-5e86-8753-ca320f4c979a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6721"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3438d77-c314-5b0a-b1f1-9ae4a3f970b6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050433}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088228} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088450} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348139} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348155} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432552}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Constant Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "79ad3266-720c-5773-97c4-728bb8e1d170" :step/math-academy-id 594359 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088814}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088814 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087874 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087601 :question/id #uuid "8ba9598f-a266-57cb-8ef5-9757dd612a34" :question/math-academy-id "e-6607"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088229} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348148} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357568} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371112} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186432553}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"} :step/id #uuid "2d09ba57-93a7-5c6d-819f-04fafb053441" :step/math-academy-id 594362 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088815}} {:db/id #edb/re